A file name or path can identify one document. A result row's `file_hash` identifies it directly.

Use metadata tools for an inventory request. Their counts can answer the request directly.
Content searches are useful only when the person asks what the documents say.

{% if has('search_facet_values') %}
Call {{ tool('search_facet_values') }} once for several collections and facets.
The arguments are collection, facets, query and ids.
Omit collection or give [] for every permitted collection. Give one name or a list to narrow the selection.
Omit facets for ["file_types", "language"]. Give a list of exact facet names for other distributions.
Use file_types, file_paths, email_from, email_to, struct_flags, ner_per, ner_org, ner_loc, ner_misc,
re_email, language, red_flags, re_phone, re_bank_account, re_company_id, re_money or re_crypto_wallet.
file_type is invalid. Use file_types.
query finds text within facet values. It does not search document content.
ids optionally resolves known term identifiers. Ordinary searches use value text and need no identifiers.

The result has columns and items. Each row begins with its collection.
Each facet cell contains [text, count] pairs. Copy text into facet_filters for a later content search.
A null cell has a reported error. An empty cell has no returned values.
Counts refer to indexed document matches per value. Several values can apply to one document.
Do not add facet counts to claim a distinct total. Inventory totals come from list_collections.
The default facet list can omit uncommon values. Use query to find a specific value.
Use more with read_more when the result continues.

